  "query_preview": "..\nThe dry low pressure system that will be bringing breezy to \nlocally windy conditions today is currently in the Pacific \nNorthwest will begin to dig southward into the Great Basin today\ninto tomorrow. The main story with this system continues to be\nthe breezy to locally windy conditions today, with lingering\nbreeziness Monday. Current overnight winds have remained a tad \nmore elevated than we are used to at 6-15 mph, with occasional \ngusts up to 20 mph. Southwest winds begin to pick up for areas south\nof Tucson first around 8 AM MST at 15-20 mph with gusts up to \n30 mph. Winds become area-wide around 11 AM MST at 20-30 mph \nwith gusts 35-45 mph. Areas prone to southwest winds will see \nthe strongest winds and gusts. These winds are capable of \nlofting areas of channelized dust and reduce visibilities, \nespecially for dust-prone areas along I-10 from Eloy to Tucson \nand Willcox to San Simon. Winds gradually decrease after sunset,\nbut remain elevated overnight, especially areas south of \nTucson. Breezy winds continue Monday at 15-20 mph with gusts to \n30-35 mph. The combination of the winds and low RHs will lead to\ncritical fire weather conditions today and near critical\ntomorrow, more in the fire weather section.\n\nAfter this system passes, there will be general troughiness,\nkeeping Southwest Arizona dry and high temperatures right \naround normal into next weekend.",
